{{!--
  Public, unauthenticated, and required by the App Store as the wallet's
  support URL. The people who most need this page are the ones who cannot sign
  in, so nothing here may sit behind a login.
--}}
<section class="page-hero page-hero--compact">
  <p class="eyebrow">Support</p>
  <h1>Get help with Aegis ID.</h1>
  <p>
    Aegis ID is an identity platform with a companion wallet on iOS and Android.
    Most questions come from one of two places — setting a wallet up, or getting
    back in after losing a device. Both are answered below.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band band--white">
  <div class="auth-grid auth-grid--compact">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Contact</p>
      <h2>Talk to somebody.</h2>
      <p>
        If your organization issued you a credential, your own administrator can
        usually resolve it faster than we can — they can reissue an invitation,
        reset a credential, or confirm who you are. Come to us when they cannot.
      </p>
      <p>
        Please include the environment you are on and, if it is a wallet
        problem, your Wallet ID. Never send a recovery code, a password, or a
        break-glass code to anybody, including us.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="auth-panel">
      <div class="auth-panel__form">
        <h2>Support</h2>
        {{#if hasSupportEmail}}
          <p><a class="button button--primary" href="mailto:{{supportEmail}}">Email {{supportEmail}}</a></p>
          <p class="form-footnote">
            We answer within one business day. There is no telephone support line.
          </p>
        {{else}}
          {{!--
            An honest empty state rather than a mailbox nobody reads. SUPPORT_EMAIL
            is unset on this deployment.
          --}}
          <p class="form-alert">
            No support address is configured for this deployment.
          </p>
          <p class="form-footnote">
            Contact the administrator at the organization that issued your
            credential, or whoever asked you to install the wallet.
          </p>
        {{/if}}

        {{#if isNonProd}}
          <p class="form-footnote">
            This is the <strong>{{deployEnv}}</strong> environment. It is for
            testing, and the credentials in it are not real.
          </p>
        {{/if}}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--light">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">The wallet</p>
    <h2>Setting one up.</h2>
    <p>
      The wallet is the holder's half of Aegis ID. It is not useful on its own —
      an organization has to issue you a credential or send you an invitation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="assurance-grid">
    <article class="assurance-card">
      <h3>Your Wallet ID</h3>
      <p>
        First run asks for an email address and gives you a Wallet ID. That is
        what you hand an administrator so they can issue a credential to your
        device.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is an identifier, not a secret. Knowing it does not let anybody act
        as you, so it is safe to send by email or read out.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="assurance-card">
      <h3>Redeeming an invitation</h3>
      <p>
        Two ways, and they are not interchangeable. <strong>Scan</strong> reads a
        QR shown on another screen. <strong>Paste invitation</strong>, on the
        Home tab, takes a <code>{{walletUrlScheme}}://</code> link that arrived
        by email or message on the phone itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the second when the invitation is on the device you are holding —
        you cannot scan a code with the screen displaying it.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="assurance-card">
      <h3>More than one organization</h3>
      <p>
        One wallet holds credentials from as many organizations as invite you.
        You do not need to install it twice.
      </p>
      <p>
        They stay separate: each organization sees only what it issued you and
        the approvals it asked for, never another organization's, and never the
        list of who else you hold a credential from.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="assurance-card">
      <h3>Nothing arrived</h3>
      <p>
        An invitation goes to the address registered in the wallet, and it can
        expire. Ask your administrator to check the address and send a fresh one.
      </p>
      <p>
        They can also issue straight to your Wallet ID, which avoids email
        entirely.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--white">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Losing a device</p>
    <h2>Getting back in.</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout--warning">
    <p>
      <strong>The key behind your wallet never leaves the device it was made
      on.</strong> It is not in any backup, so a new phone cannot restore the old
      wallet — recovery issues a new key rather than recovering the old one.
      That is why the ten single-use recovery codes shown during setup matter,
      and why they are shown only once.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="assurance-grid">
    <article class="assurance-card">
      <h3>You still have your recovery codes</h3>
      <p>
        Install the wallet on the new device, choose <em>Recover an existing
        wallet</em>, and use one code. Your credentials and your organizations
        come back and the code is spent.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="assurance-card">
      <h3>You have run out of codes</h3>
      <p>
        This is a deliberate hard stop rather than a bug. An administrator has to
        verify who you are away from the system and authorise a re-enrolment.
        Contact yours.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="assurance-card">
      <h3>You administer an organization</h3>
      <p>
        If you have lost the authenticator you sign in with, and your
        organization has root wallets, two of their holders can approve your
        recovery at <a href="/auth/recover/approvals">/auth/recover/approvals</a>
        with no platform operator involved.
      </p>
      <p>
        Otherwise start at <a href="/auth/recover">/auth/recover</a>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="assurance-card">
      <h3>Your organization has lost every root wallet</h3>
      <p>
        Break-glass recovery covers it, and needs both the code your organization
        generated and kept, and a platform administrator. Nobody here can reach
        your organization without it.
      </p>
      <p>Contact support with your ticket reference.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--light">
  <div class="auth-grid auth-grid--compact">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h2>What the wallet holds.</h2>
      <p>
        An email address and, if you gave one, a phone number — both entered by
        you, and used only so an organization can address a credential to the
        right person. There is no analytics and no tracking.
      </p>
      <p>
        Credentials and key material stay on the device. To have your wallet and
        its record removed, ask the organization that issued to you, or contact
        support.
      </p>
      {{#if hasPrivacyPolicy}}
        <p><a href="{{privacyPolicyUrl}}">Read the full privacy policy</a></p>
      {{/if}}
    </div>

    <div class="auth-panel">
      <div class="auth-panel__form">
        <h2>Getting the app</h2>
        {{!--
          The published listings come first and exist on production only. On
          dev and qa the deploy script blanks them, so what remains is the test
          build for the environment you are actually on — which is the one that
          talks to this site.
        --}}
        {{#if hasAppStore}}
          <p><a class="button button--secondary" href="{{appStoreUrl}}">iOS — App Store</a></p>
        {{/if}}
        {{#if hasPlayStore}}
          <p><a class="button button--secondary" href="{{playStoreUrl}}">Android — Google Play</a></p>
        {{/if}}
        {{#if hasIosTestFlight}}
          <p><a class="button button--secondary" href="{{iosTestFlightUrl}}">iOS — TestFlight</a></p>
        {{/if}}
        {{#if hasAndroidDownload}}
          <p><a class="button button--secondary" href="/downloads/android">Android — install guide</a></p>
        {{/if}}
        <p><a class="button button--secondary" href="/docs/tutorial/get-started-guide.html">Get Started Guide</a></p>

        <h2>Reporting a security issue</h2>
        <p class="form-footnote">
          {{#if hasSupportEmail}}
            Write to <a href="mailto:{{supportEmail}}">{{supportEmail}}</a> with
            "security" in the subject. Please tell us before telling anybody
            else, and give us a reasonable window to fix it.
          {{else}}
            No support address is configured for this deployment. Report through
            your organization's administrator.
          {{/if}}
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
